  "provenance": [
    {
      "source_type": "manufacturer-pdf",
      "source": "Topps official checklist PDF: 2020_20Topps_20Baseball_20Series_201_20Checklist.pdf",
      "retrieved": "2026-08-07",
      "covers": "Series 1 (#1-350) and its inserts",
      "notes": "Topps prints \"CHicago Cubs\" on #119, which is why that form is in teams.json. Eight programmes run in both series and are kept per series, their names carrying the wave."
    },
    {
      "source_type": "manufacturer-pdf",
      "source": "Topps official checklist PDF: 2020_20Topps_20Series_202_20Baseball_20Checklist_20Final.pdf",
      "retrieved": "2026-08-07",
      "covers": "Series 2 (#351-700) and its inserts",
      "notes": "Seventeen sections headed AUTOGRAPH PARALLEL share no card number with any other file in this set — Decades Best autographs run BD70-BB where the insert runs DB-1 — so every one is an autograph checklist numbering itself and none declares varies. The question was settled by the numbers, not by the word parallel in the header. Six CHROME VARIATION and BOXTOPPER VARIATION sections are inserts for the same reason, running 85C, DBC, TRC and OTR against their parents' 85B, DB and TR. The exception is the 1985 Topps Baseball Chrome autographs, whose 46 numbers all appear in the 50-card chrome list and name the same players, so they vary it. Decades Best Chrome prints team-only cards: \"DBC-1 Detroit Tigers Detroit Tigers\" names the team twice while DBC-3 and DBC-5 name it once and leave the subject implied."
    }
  ]
